{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>and behold, a ram — another — caught in the thicket by its horns.</b> The word 'אַחַר' implies as though there were two rams here, and Scripture wished to distinguish between them and said that this is a different ram from the first. For even though according to this it should have been pointed with a tzere under the ḥet, nevertheless one may explain it as though it is pointed with a tzere, since a pataḥ under the ḥet does not sit so well, given that the word 'אַחַר' is written between 'אַיִל' and 'נֶאֱחַז'. And there are those who say that this ram is not from among those rams that were created during the six days of Genesis, but it is a different ram, for the ram of Isaac was created at twilight on the eve of Sabbath (Avot 5:8)." "And what seems closest to me to say regarding this is that every person who is a sinner and rebellious is likened to a man possessing horns, goring upward — regarding transgressions between man and the Blessed HaShem, as Rashi explained in parashat Lekh Lekha (Genesis 14:2) on 'מֵאֵבֶר' — 'שֵׂם אֵבֶר' — to leap and to fly upward. And so regarding the nations it is said (Daniel 8:20) 'הָאַיִל אֲשֶׁר רָאִיתָ בַּעַל הַקְּרָנַיִם' etc. Thus the parable was drawn here regarding this ram possessing horns, for there is one that gores upward — this is the one who sins against Heaven, and most horns of animals turn to the right and to the left, for so does a man who sins against his fellow harm him — whether through the length of days on his right, whether through wealth and honor on his left. Therefore the first man, who was alone in the world and sinned against Heaven alone, had available for his atonement an ox that had a single horn on its forehead — meaning that by virtue of being on his forehead it pointed upward, for in that wherein he sinned, he shall make rectification. And from this our Sages of blessed memory learned (Ḥullin 60a) to say likewise, that it had a single horn and specifically on its forehead. But for later generations, every sinner brings an ox or a sheep or a goat possessing horns to the right and to the left, to atone for that wherein he sinned against his fellow — for even one who sins in matters between man and the Blessed HaShem nevertheless also harms created beings: both because all Israel are guarantors for one another, and many bore the punishment of his sin as is known; and because he causes others to learn to do as his deeds do. Therefore it is fitting for him to bring an animal possessing horns to the right and to the left, to atone for what he sinned against created beings, and the Holy One, blessed be He, forgives His portion — for if his transgressions are many, what shall the Blessed One do to him? But against a man like himself he could have dealt a great blow according to the magnitude of his power; therefore their laws differ in offerings: the anointed priest and the Sanhedrin — their power is great and it was in their hand to deal a great blow against their fellows, therefore their law is to bring a bull, whose goring is great and most dangerous. And any ordinary individual — his goring against his fellow is not so great, and he resembles a sheep or a goat, whose goring is not so great, therefore their law is to bring a sheep or a goat. But the poor person, whose power is too weak to cause harm, resembles a bird that strikes with its wings in the manner of 'מֵאֵבֶר' — therefore his law is to bring turtledoves or young pigeons, to say that he is pursued like them and even so he strikes with his wings against them. And in the utmost poverty, the person is considered like a dead person, therefore his offering is fine flour that has no living soul. And this will be explained further with the help of HaShem (Leviticus 4), because every thing that atones must resemble in its features the one being atoned for. This is what it says (Psalms 75:5–6): 'אָמַרְתִּי לַהוֹלְלִים אַל תָּהֹלּוּ וְלָרְשָׁעִים אַל תָּרִימוּ קָרֶן, אַל תָּרִימוּ לַמָּרוֹם קַרְנְכֶם'. 'אַל תָּרִימוּ קָרֶן' — one horn in meaning: that they should not sin against Heaven alone. 'אַל תָּרִימוּ לַמָּרוֹם קַרְנְכֶם' — two in meaning, corresponding to one who sins also against man. Therefore it says 'אַל תָּרִימוּ לַמָּרוֹם' — two raisings, one to the right and one to the left as stated." "And this matter was hinted to Abraham, for there is no man righteous on earth who does good and does not sin, and all the more so according to the opinion of those who say (Nedarim 32a) that the Egyptian exile was on account of Abraham's sin when he said 'בַּמָּה אֵדָע' (Genesis 15:8), and he caused his children affliction and servitude and the death of the children in Egypt, as though he had been goring at them right and left. Therefore it was fitting for him to offer 'אַיִל אַחֵר' — another ram, and it is 'another' than the first, for the first ram is the sinful man who gores northward and southward, and in HaShem's compassion He took as his substitute a ram that was caught in the thicket by its horns. The matter of its being 'caught in the thicket' — that is to say, in a tree — hints that thus the sinner becomes entangled in sin that stems from eating from the Tree of Knowledge, which was the cause of all sin. And the matter of 'בְּקַרְנָיו' — by its horns — is that the horns of the sinner caused him to become entangled in sin, as though it said that the man was caught and seized in the snare because re'em-like horns were his, lifting a horn on high or to the right and to the left. And in Bereshit Rabbah (56:9) they said: 'וְהִנֵּה אַיִל אַחַר' — what is 'אַחַר'? All the days, Israel is caught in transgressions and entangled in troubles, and in the end they will be redeemed through the horn of the ram. Behold, the plain sense of this midrash agrees with our words, that this verse speaks of a sinner and a rebel whose horns are from his own hand. And what it said 'and in the end they will be redeemed through the horn of the ram' — it appears to me to explain this regarding the shofar of Rosh HaShanah, which is from the horn of a ram, and through it Israel will be redeemed from the hand of their evil inclination, whose snare is spread over all the living, and through the shofar, which comes to confound the Satan, they will be redeemed from his hand, for the shofar stirs a person to repentance, as it is written (Amos 3:6) 'אִם יִתָּקַע שׁוֹפָר בְּעִיר וְהָעָם לֹא יֶחֱרָדוּ'." "And what our Sages of blessed memory said (Rosh HaShanah 16b) that the shofar confounds the Satan — from the standpoint of his supposing that perhaps it is the shofar of the Messiah — the Satan is not so devoid of understanding, for would he not know that it is a statute for Israel from days of old and still the Messiah has not come? And it would be near to saying, since he has threshed [i.e., made the same deduction many times before]? Rather, he knows that it is the commandment of HaShem upon us, and he also knows the reason for the commandment, which is to stir [us] to repentance, and repentance draws near the redemption. Therefore he is alarmed lest the Messiah come, for even though in the previous years they were not stirred to repentance as they should have been, nevertheless there will eventually come a time when they shall regret their wickedness and do complete repentance. Therefore he is afraid every single year lest now they tremble and return. And because the horn is the place of the sin, therefore it will also be the place of the rectification — a model of the fig leaves of the first man, for in that wherein he corrupted, he was rectified. So too with the horn, through it came the corruption and through it will come the rectification, and the prosecutor will be turned into the defender, for every master of repentance — his willful sins are transformed for him into merits. Thus this horn is the place of willful sins, and through it he will come to the merits."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The comment discusses whether the word אַחַר should be vocalized with a pataḥ (as it appears in the text) or with a tzere (meaning 'another'). The Kli Yakar argues that despite the pataḥ vocalization, the word functions as though pointed with a tzere, i.e., 'another.'", 1 "Rashi on Genesis 14:2 explains the word מֵאֵבֶר as cognate with אֵבֶר (a limb/organ), as a wing used to leap upward — the image of a horn pointing upward. The Kli Yakar applies the same imagery to horns pointing left and right.", 2 "The Kli Yakar reads 'northward and southward' (צָפוֹנָה וָנֶגְבָּה) as parallel to the 'right and left' (יָמִין וּשְׂמֹאל) of the previous comment, following biblical orientation where north = right and south = left.", 3 "The phrase 'כֵיוָן דְּדָשׁ דָּשׁ' is an Aramaic idiom meaning 'since he has repeated this inference many times' — i.e., the Satan has drawn the same false conclusion year after year. Rendered literally per the obscure-text protocol."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:29:42.790310381Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}